  "topic": "exit function",
  "explanation": "The exit() function in C is a standard library function that immediately terminates the calling process. It is used to end program execution gracefully or with an error indication, allowing the program to return a status code to the operating system, which helps in debugging and script automation by signaling success (0) or failure (non-zero).",
  "syntax": "void exit(int status);",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <stdlib.h>",
    "int main() {",
    "    exit(0);",
    "    return 0;  // This line is unreachable",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This example demonstrates the exit() function by calling it in the main function with a status code of 0, which terminates the program immediately upon execution. As a result, any code after the exit() call, like the return statement, is never reached, showing how exit() abruptly ends the program and returns the specified status to the system."
}
